.idea/
.claude/settings.local.json
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# draw.io editor backups (.$<name>.drawio.bkp)
.$*.bkp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUN OUTPUTS — ALL OF THEM, WITHOUT EXCEPTION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repository is public and holds the reusable half of SiteMapper: maps,
# schemas, workflows and scripts. Run outputs live in the private companion
# repo, SiteMapper-data, whose layout mirrors this one.
#
# It is a blanket rule on purpose. The previous version listed directories
# one at a time with a per-case justification, and the failure mode was
# structural: a NEW project's results were committed by default, and the
# question "is this one sensitive?" got asked after the data was already
# published. Excluding everything and moving the whole class out inverts that —
# a run output has to be deliberately carried across, not deliberately caught.
#
# No negations. An earlier version kept results/README.md via a contents-glob
# plus "!results/README.md", which is fragile enough to have needed its own
# comment explaining why. Layout docs belong beside the workflow, not inside a
# directory that is otherwise entirely excluded.
results/
**/results/

# The private data repo, if it is cloned inside this tree rather than beside it.
SiteMapper-data/

# Identifying inputs that are NOT run outputs, and so would not be caught above.
# This repository is the FRAMEWORK: schema, docs, scripts, and one worked example
# in sites/sitemapper-demo. Real maps belong to whoever mapped them and live in
# their own repository, together with the runs they produce.
#
#   config.yaml    keeps settings.policy only. settings.contact and
#                  settings.mailboxes name a real person, so a deployment keeps
#                  them in its own private config and merges them in at global
#                  scope. See schema/settings.yaml.
#
# Nothing tenant-specific is listed by name here any more, because nothing
# tenant-specific is supposed to be in this tree at all.
